WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.040 --> 00:00:04.720
Cruella de Vil gets a fashionable&nbsp;
transformation in Disney's latest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.720 --> 00:00:08.160
live-action reimagining of one&nbsp;
of its most iconic villains.
&nbsp;

00:00:08.160 --> 00:00:12.960
Yippee-ki-yay, movie lovers, I'm Jan and today&nbsp;
I'm explaining the biggest changes Disney made in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.960 --> 00:00:19.120
Cruella and how the new film compares to both the&nbsp;
animated and live-action 101 Dalmatians movies.
&nbsp;

00:00:19.120 --> 00:00:21.680
Some spoilers for the new&nbsp;
film ahead, so take care.
&nbsp;

00:00:21.680 --> 00:00:25.760
"I'm just getting started, darling."
Because this is an origin story, there's much&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.760 --> 00:00:30.640
more to the character of Cruella here than we see&nbsp;
in the 101 Dalmatians movies where she arrives as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.640 --> 00:00:34.000
an already fully-formed villain,
owning every scene she's in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.000 --> 00:00:38.800
with extravagantly theatrical flair.
In fact, as much as I love dogs, Glenn Close&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.800 --> 00:00:43.600
hamming it up deliciously in the remake is the&nbsp;
only thing that made it worth watching for me.
&nbsp;

00:00:43.600 --> 00:00:47.680
As Cruella gets the full spotlight this&nbsp;
time around, rather than the Dalmatians,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.680 --> 00:00:50.480
her personality and background&nbsp;
are suitably fleshed out,
&nbsp;

00:00:50.480 --> 00:00:54.160
with nice nods to the original&nbsp;
films via backstories for her name,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.160 --> 00:00:59.680
style, crazy driving, henchmen, and more.
In a 180-degree turn from her previous&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.680 --> 00:01:03.840
incarnations though, this Cruella –&nbsp;
who starts life known as Estella – even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.840 --> 00:01:10.080
likes canines, not as future fur coats but&nbsp;
as actual much-loved animal companions.
&nbsp;

00:01:10.080 --> 00:01:15.520
Cruella is still all about the drama though,&nbsp;
darling, and Emma Stone does a striking job&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.520 --> 00:01:19.200
turning her dramatic AF dial up to 11.
"Do you have a light?"
&nbsp;

00:01:22.080 --> 00:01:26.400
A brand new character we meet that's central&nbsp;
to Estella's journey to becoming Cruella is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.400 --> 00:01:31.040
Baroness von Hellman who's head of her&nbsp;
own highly successful fashion house.
&nbsp;

00:01:31.040 --> 00:01:35.120
The Baroness is essentially what you'd&nbsp;
get if you took the OG Cruella de Vil&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.120 --> 00:01:40.880
from 101 Dalmatians and Miranda Priestly from&nbsp;
A Devil Wears Prada and rolled them into one;
&nbsp;

00:01:40.880 --> 00:01:44.480
and Emma Thompson plays her&nbsp;
to quietly savage perfection.
&nbsp;

00:01:44.480 --> 00:01:48.880
"You're sweating and I can smell you."
When she's not taking credit for everyone&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.880 --> 00:01:50.720
else's work,
"Here's to me"
&nbsp;

00:01:50.720 --> 00:01:55.680
the Baroness is busy showing her utter contempt&nbsp;
for the rest of humanity. "That's marvellous."
&nbsp;

00:01:55.680 --> 00:01:59.440
But this narcissist who treats&nbsp;
pretty much everyone like dogs,
&nbsp;

00:01:59.440 --> 00:02:01.760
"Estella, come."
has got skeletons&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.760 --> 00:02:06.000
as well as couture in her closet.
And as characters that represent a clash&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.000 --> 00:02:11.120
between the old-fashioned establishment and the&nbsp;
cutting-edge future, Thompson and Stone play off&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.120 --> 00:02:14.880
each other to truly wicked effect.
"1965 collection."
&nbsp;

00:02:14.880 --> 00:02:16.800
"Oh!
No wonder I love it, it's mine."
&nbsp;

00:02:16.800 --> 00:02:20.080
"I fixed it."  
The three Dalmatians owned by the Baroness&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.080 --> 00:02:26.480
in this movie are a world away from the sweet and&nbsp;
friendly ones in both the 1961 and 1996 films.
&nbsp;

00:02:26.480 --> 00:02:29.840
As the Baroness herself says,&nbsp;
they're "gorgeous and vicious",
&nbsp;

00:02:29.840 --> 00:02:33.760
"It's my favourite combination."
And, oh boy, she's not kidding! 
&nbsp;

00:02:33.760 --> 00:02:39.120
It's hardly surprising these dogs act so vicious&nbsp;
though, after all they do belong to the Baroness,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.120 --> 00:02:43.520
and she's viciousness personified. 
The Dalmatians appear in several crucial&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.520 --> 00:02:48.080
scenes that point at why Cruella feels the way&nbsp;
she does about the breed in the original films,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.080 --> 00:02:53.200
but unlike those movies, this isn't their&nbsp;
story, so, expect to see less of them here.
&nbsp;

00:02:53.200 --> 00:02:58.000
However, Cruella and friends both have their&nbsp;
own adorably scrappy pooches, Buddy and Wink,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.000 --> 00:03:01.840
that join them on their adventures, so&nbsp;
there's still plenty of canine shenanigans&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.840 --> 00:03:05.200
to enjoy throughout the story.
In a change from the animation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.200 --> 00:03:09.040
but just like the live-action remake,&nbsp;
there's no talking animals here.
&nbsp;

00:03:09.040 --> 00:03:14.560
And the animals you do see on screen are a mix of&nbsp;
real-life pups and CGI, which is similar to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.560 --> 00:03:21.520
90s film, though that movie also used puppets.
In contrast to the 60s animation and 90s flick&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.520 --> 00:03:23.680
which were both set around&nbsp;
the time they were made,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.680 --> 00:03:29.280
Cruella's new origin story eschews a contemporary&nbsp;
setting, opting instead for the 1970s,
&nbsp;

00:03:29.280 --> 00:03:32.800
which is the perfect backdrop&nbsp;
for her anarchic attitude.
&nbsp;

00:03:32.800 --> 00:03:36.400
The culture clash of that particular&nbsp;
period plays out beautifully in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.400 --> 00:03:41.840
contrasting styles of the Baroness and Cruella.
The Baroness has shades of establishment fashion&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.840 --> 00:03:47.760
houses like Dior about her versus Cruella's&nbsp;
more punk sensibilities of Vivienne Westwood.
&nbsp;

00:03:47.760 --> 00:03:52.560
But this isn't the first time Cruella's worked&nbsp;
in the realm of high-fashion, as she ran her&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.560 --> 00:03:58.000
own company, House of De Vil, in the 90s.
Her OTT personal style, which was mostly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.000 --> 00:04:02.240
two-toned, with some flamboyant splashes of&nbsp;
colour thrown in, certainly stood out in that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.240 --> 00:04:07.760
movie and fit her big personality, building as&nbsp;
it did on her iconic look in the animation.  
&nbsp;

00:04:07.760 --> 00:04:12.640
However, this latest film's fashion-led&nbsp;
storyline takes things to a whole new level,
&nbsp;

00:04:12.640 --> 00:04:18.800
giving us an outrageously tailored world&nbsp;
where Emma Stone has 47 wildly inventive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.800 --> 00:04:25.280
costume changes, Emma Thompson an elegantly&nbsp;
luxurious 33, with Joel Fry and Paul Walter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.280 --> 00:04:30.160
Hauser getting 30 changes each too.
It certainly looks like costume designer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.160 --> 00:04:34.160
Jenny Beavan will be back at the Oscars&nbsp;
accepting another little gold statue as she&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.160 --> 00:04:38.320
did most recently for Mad Max: Fury Road
because this stunning display of costume&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.320 --> 00:04:42.000
design from her and her team&nbsp;
is going to take some topping!
&nbsp;

00:04:42.000 --> 00:04:48.080
By the way, curiously, the new film's timeline&nbsp;
where Cruella is in her 20s in the 1970s seems&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.080 --> 00:04:52.800
to fit with the timeline of the first live-action&nbsp;
film where Glenn Close was in her 40s.
&nbsp;

00:04:53.440 --> 00:04:58.000
On top of giving us Cruella's backstory,&nbsp;
the new movie also provides an origin&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.000 --> 00:05:02.480
for her henchmen, Jasper and Horace.
In this version, they first meet as kids on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:02.480 --> 00:05:07.280
streets of London and grow up grifting together,&nbsp;
so they're basically Cruella's family and look out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.280 --> 00:05:11.680
for one another, rather than just being the&nbsp;
hired heavies we know in the other films. 
&nbsp;

00:05:11.680 --> 00:05:15.680
In fact, for director Craig Gillespie,&nbsp;
Jasper is "the heart of the film",&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.680 --> 00:05:20.640
"the touchstone for Cruella", as he tries to&nbsp;
stop her going too far down the wrong path.
&nbsp;

00:05:20.640 --> 00:05:24.960
But as Cruella indulges her wicked side&nbsp;
more and more, her relationship with her&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.960 --> 00:05:28.320
chosen family becomes strained:
"Is it so hard to back me up?"
&nbsp;

00:05:28.320 --> 00:05:31.680
"Not Estella, that's easy, but to&nbsp;
help Cruella, it's a nightmare."
&nbsp;

00:05:31.680 --> 00:05:36.720
Joel Fry and Paul Walter Hauser work well&nbsp;
together in the roles, and while Fry's Jasper&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:36.720 --> 00:05:39.440
is the one with insight here.
"She has no family."
&nbsp;

00:05:39.440 --> 00:05:42.320
That's a change from the animation&nbsp;
where Horace was the one who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:42.320 --> 00:05:47.120
generally understood what was going on.
"What if they went down the froze-up creek,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:47.120 --> 00:05:50.480
so's not to leave their tracks?"
"Horace, you idiot!
&nbsp;

00:05:50.480 --> 00:05:54.080
Dogs ain't that smart."
Interestingly, Hauser, who plays Horace,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:54.080 --> 00:05:58.720
has compared the characters to Bert and Ernie;
and told Comicbook.com that he pinched "Bob&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:58.720 --> 00:06:03.040
Hoskins' voice from the movie Hook" for the part.
"How can you not love this face?"
&nbsp;

00:06:03.040 --> 00:06:08.240
As for the 90s take on Jasper and Horace,&nbsp;
well, that really lays the slapstick on thick,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:08.240 --> 00:06:11.440
turning the pair into knock-offs&nbsp;
of the Home Alone baddies,
&nbsp;

00:06:11.440 --> 00:06:15.840
which makes sense when you realise it was written&nbsp;
by John Hughes who also wrote Home Alone.
&nbsp;

00:06:16.400 --> 00:06:20.240
While the latest movie does follow the&nbsp;
original book and animation by making&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:20.240 --> 00:06:24.880
Anita and Cruella former school friends,&nbsp;
it breaks away from the 1996 film where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:24.880 --> 00:06:29.360
Anita worked as a designer for Cruella,
and instead makes her a photo-journalist.
&nbsp;

00:06:29.360 --> 00:06:33.600
Given how good actress Kirby Howell-Baptiste&nbsp;
has been in other shows, she never gets&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:33.600 --> 00:06:38.480
much to do here, which is a real shame.
Looking back, there was little of interest in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:38.480 --> 00:06:42.800
the character of Anita in either of the earlier&nbsp;
films, but that was more understandable in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:42.800 --> 00:06:48.160
animation given its short run-time and the fact&nbsp;
the Dalmatians, not the humans, were the stars.
&nbsp;

00:06:48.160 --> 00:06:52.640
When it came to the first live-action though,&nbsp;
they somehow managed to make Anita even more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:52.640 --> 00:06:57.280
bland than the animation, giving her a&nbsp;
stereotypical 50s housewife vibe despite&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:57.280 --> 00:07:01.680
the film being set in the mid-90s. 
This time around, Roger, who's played&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:01.680 --> 00:07:06.960
by Kayvan Novak, only has a small role as&nbsp;
well: he's the Baroness's lawyer and also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:06.960 --> 00:07:10.800
plays piano 'in a dingy little bar',
in a nod to the animation where he's a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:10.800 --> 00:07:15.040
songwriter and, with his wife Anita,&nbsp;
owner of the titular Dalmatians.
&nbsp;

00:07:15.040 --> 00:07:20.000
As for the 90s film, where he was a video&nbsp;
game designer, Roger was pretty dull&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:20.000 --> 00:07:24.960
and actor Jeff Daniels and on-screen wife&nbsp;
Joely Richardson had almost no chemistry.
&nbsp;

00:07:24.960 --> 00:07:29.840
In the new story, Roger and Anita never meet,&nbsp;
but stick around for the mid-credits scene&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:29.840 --> 00:07:33.280
as it features some easter eggs and&nbsp;
hints at their future together.
&nbsp;

00:07:33.840 --> 00:07:38.000
A couple of other new characters added to&nbsp;
the mix are Mark Strong's John the Valet&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:38.000 --> 00:07:42.080
and Artie played by John McCrea.
As a close confidant to the Baroness,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:42.080 --> 00:07:47.120
John knows many of her secrets and, as such,&nbsp;
plays a small but significant role in the story.
&nbsp;

00:07:47.120 --> 00:07:53.760
As for Artie, he's the LGBTQ+ owner of a vintage&nbsp;
clothes boutique and helps Cruella in her insane&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:53.760 --> 00:07:56.478
fashion stunts to steal the Baroness's thunder.
"I want to make art Artie.
&nbsp;

00:07:56.478 --> 00:07:56.582
And I want to make trouble.
You in?
&nbsp;

00:07:56.582 --> 00:07:57.520
I do love trouble."
And according to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:57.520 --> 00:08:02.640
channel Mama's Geeky, director Craig Gillespie&nbsp;
loved McCrea's witty performance so much they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:02.640 --> 00:08:07.600
ended up adding an extra scene for him! 
So, what do you think of Disney's new Cruella?
&nbsp;

00:08:07.600 --> 00:08:10.000
Do you like the changes to&nbsp;
her and the other characters?
&nbsp;

00:08:11.680 --> 00:08:14.560
Let me know your thoughts and&nbsp;
theories in the comments below.
&nbsp;

00:08:14.560 --> 00:08:17.440
If you enjoyed this, then hit&nbsp;
that like button and tap left&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:17.440 --> 00:08:21.200
for my next Disney video or tap right&nbsp;
for something else you're sure to like.
&nbsp;

00:08:21.200 --> 00:08:25.840
Thanks for watching and see ya next time.
Yippee-ki-yay, movie lovers!

